                                                                    91-11-26
                    User's Manual for the ULog utility.


1. Introduction

   Have you ever wondered if there isn't way for AmiExpress Users to send
   a message to the next user, like in many other BBS programs? Or have
   ever wanted to know more about your MsgBase, like how many letters
   are actually private? Or are you just tired of the look of your current
   Top Uploaders Util? Well, then I can guarantee the my ULog Util will
   satisfy you completely. To describe exactly what the ULog program
   does I can say this; it checks for the ten best uploaders, writes
   they're name, location, bytes uploaded, times called, and messages
   written, in a straight row. It also lets you include a headfile, that
   will be located at the top of the Outputfile. Then the Total megs
   uploaded and downloaded are written down. As are the Total number of
   users, the Total number of calls to the system, the total amount of
   messages written, and the average computertype on your board. The next
   thing written to the output file is the User Message. This is the
   Message to the next user which I was talking about earlier. The
   Utility checks your selected MsgBase for letters written to a user
   called 'ULog', so you must therefore log a user with this name in.
   Access is of no concern, it will never actually use the account.
   Only look for that name in the MsgBase. If a message to 'ULog' is
   found it centers it, and writes it down in the outputfile with the rest.
   While checking for the ULog message it also remembers various data about
   the MsgBase which it later writes down along with the time the program
   was executed. I think that just about covers it, so let's get down to
   business.

   Features of ULog:

   * Shows the top 10 uploaders, with this info for each uploader:
       > Name
       > Location
       > Uploaded bytes
       > Calls to the system
       > Messages posted.
   * Shows the total amount of users.
   * Shows the total amount of calls to the system.
   * Shows the total number of megs uploaded to the system.
   * Shows the total number of megs downloaded from the system.
   * Shows the total number of messages written in the system.
   * Shows the average computer type of the users.
   * Informs you what time it was executed at.
   * Displays a user message. (Message to the next user)
   * Shows various information about the MessageBase, such as;
     total number of messages, total number of private messages,
     total number of public messages, total number of deleted
     messages.
   * Posibility for the sysop to specify the number of uploaders
     that are supposed to be shown. (New in this version)
   * Ability to import Environment Variables from LogBook.
   * All versions are tested on well know boards before release, to
     prevent all bugs.
   * All versions are fully compatible with all Amiga models and OS's.
     (See below for a full description on the hardware and software
     setups used when testing this product)


   News in this version

   Ability to disinclude the MsgBase info, and ability to import
   Environment Variables from LogBook.

2. How to get started

   The usage of the ULog util looks like this:

   ULog [UserData] [Output] [-g/-n] [IncludeFile/-n] [MsgBase/-n]
   [-y/-n] [-y/-n] {NumULs}

   The User.Data filename and complete path must be specified. The same
   goes for the Outputfile. (This is were the util will write to.) You
   may specify an '*' sign as the outputfilename and the util will write
   to the current CLI/Shell window instead of a file. The -g/-n option
   is for, whether you want ANSI graphics/codes in the outputfile or not.
   Select '-g' for ANSI graphics, or '-n' for NO ANSI graphics. The
   Includefile/-n option is put to enable you to include a textfile
   before the actual top uploaders list. Enter the complete filename and
   path, to use this function, or just -n to skip it. The next option is
   the MsgBase Path/-n option. This is if you want ULog to search for
   messages for the next user, written to 'ULog', and in that case, where
   to look for it. Please note that you should NOT write the actual file
   name of the MsgBase. Just path representing it. For example; let's
   say you have a directory for the board on your harddrive called 'BBS'.
   This directory is assigned, so you only need to write 'BBS:', and
   you're in. Then let's say you have a conference directory in there,
   which is called 'Elite'. Then, of course, you have a directory inside
   the elite directory called 'MsgBase' which contains the actual
   HeaderFile. The path entered to the ULog util would then be;

   BBS:Elite/MsgBase/

   Pretty neat and simple, eh? The first -y/-n option lets you choose
   wether you want the MsgBase info or not. -y for info, and -n for
   no info. The second -y/-n specifies wether ULog should try to find
   Environment Variables left by LogBook or not. LogBook 1.6 always
   leaves these variables so, make sure you run ULog after LogBook
   in that case. If you choose -y, and the Environment Variables are
   found you will see who the last caller was in the output file of
   ULog. If you choose -n, nothing will appear. This function was
   suggested to me by BamBam/Classic simple because he doesn't have
   his LogBook as the first textfile when you log in. His LogBook
   is in the Bulletins, and Ulog is the first thing that you see.
   I therefor included this function, since there might be others
   who would like to do the same thing.

                       The 'NumULs' option does not have to be
   entered, but if you do, you should enter a number between 1 and 50,
   and the ULog will show the number of uploaders that you specified,
   instead of the usual 10. If no number is entered here, ULog will
   use the default of 10. Ok, then let's move along to a complete
   example. Let's assume that the conditions for the MessageBase are the
   same as above, and your User.Data file is placed in the BBS: directory.
   The desired outputfile is in the Elite Directory, and is called;
   'Bull20.txt'. You would also like to have the outputfile done with
   ANSI. You have previously created a textfile which you also want
   included before the actual Top Uploaders List, and this file is
   placed in the S: directory and is called 'Head'. You would like
   all the MsgBase info, and you would like ULog to search for LogBook's
   Environment Variables. The number of uploaders you would like to
   be displays is 15. The complete commandline would the look like this:

   ULog BBS:User.Data BBS:Elite/Bull20.txt -g S:Head BBS:Elite/MsgBase/
   -y -y 15

   If you, on the other hand, would like to run the most simple version
   of ULog output, the commandline might look something like this.

   ULog BBS:User.Data BBS:Elite/Bull20.txt -n -n -n -n -n

   This means; no ANSI graphics, no includefile, no user message, no
   mailstats, no LogBook Environment Variable search and just 10 uploaders.
   It all depends on what you like, when you choose you're commandline.


3. Hints

   The reason for which I put in the option to select the name and path
   of the User.Data file, was so that you can copy it to RAM: and let
   execute from there. This might give greater preformance depending on
   what kind of HardDisk you use. You can also do the same thing with
   the outputfile, although I doubt it will make any difference. Copying
   the HeaderFile to RAM: won't work, since the program will also need
   to access the actual files containing the letters, which are placed
   in the same directory as the HeaderFile.

   I strongly suggest that you execute this utility after every logoff,
   since the 'Message to the next user' function would be pretty useless,
   if it wasn't updated after every logoff.

   Instead of executing the util once for ANSI graphics, and once for
   no ANSI graphics, use the small util included in this archive called
   'NoAnsi', which will work as a copy command, but the outputfile will
   be stripped from all ANSI graphics. This will be much faster that
   executing ULog twice.


4. Versions

   In this release I have included two version of my util:

   ULog1.9     - Top Uploaders.
   DLog1.9     - Top Downloaders.

   DLog is the same thing as ULog, except DLog creates a list of
   the Top Ten Downloaders, instead of uploaders, and you want
   to be able to send user messages to DLog you must also
   log in a user called 'DLog'.


